<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-more-markov" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Long-Run Behaviour: Two More Examples</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      When a matrix is diagonalizable, writing the initial state in a basis of
      eigenvectors makes the long-run behaviour transparent: the term belonging to the
      largest eigenvalue dominates, and everything else decays.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <example xml:id="eg-truck-rental">
    <title>Truck Rentals</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        A truck rental company has <m>3</m> locations, where you can rent moving trucks.
        You can return them to any other location. For simplicity, suppose that every
        customer returns their truck the next day. Let <m>V_k</m> be the vector whose
        entries are the number of trucks in locations <m>1</m>, <m>2</m>, and <m>3</m>,
        respectively. Let <m>A</m> be the matrix whose <m>(i,j)</m>-entry is the
        probability that a customer renting a truck from location <m>j</m> returns it to
        location <m>i</m>. Consider
        <md>
          A = \begin{bmatrix}
            0.3 \amp 0.4 \amp 0.5 \\
            0.3 \amp 0.4 \amp 0.3 \\
            0.4 \amp 0.2 \amp 0.2
          \end{bmatrix}.
        </md>
        Assume that initially the trucks are distributed in the <m>3</m> locations as
        <m>\vec{V}_0 = \begin{bmatrix} 7 \\ 3 \\ 8 \end{bmatrix}</m>, and find out the
        percentages of trucks that will be distributed in each location in the long run.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Expanding <m>\det(A - \lambda I) = 0</m> and factoring gives
        <md>
          -(\lambda + 0.2)(1 - \lambda)(0.1 - \lambda) = 0,
          \qquad \text{so} \qquad
          \lambda_1 = 1, \quad \lambda_2 = -0.2, \quad \lambda_3 = 0.1.
        </md>
        Here <m>\lambda_1 = 1</m> is the <term>dominant eigenvalue</term>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>For <m>\lambda_1 = 1</m>:</term> row reducing
        <m>(A - I)\vec{x} = \vec{0}</m> gives
        <md>
          \vec{x} = \begin{bmatrix} \frac{7}{5}t \\ \frac{6}{5}t \\ t \end{bmatrix}
          = \frac{t}{5} \begin{bmatrix} 7 \\ 6 \\ 5 \end{bmatrix},
          \qquad \text{so} \qquad
          \vec{x}_1 = \begin{bmatrix} 7 \\ 6 \\ 5 \end{bmatrix}.
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        Similarly, for the other eigenvalues we find
        <md>
          \vec{x}_2 = \begin{bmatrix} -1 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} \text{ for } \lambda_2 = -0.2,
          \qquad
          \vec{x}_3 = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ -3 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix} \text{ for } \lambda_3 = 0.1.
        </md>
        The set <m>\{ \vec{x}_1, \vec{x}_2, \vec{x}_3 \}</m> is linearly independent since
        the <m>\vec{x}_i</m> are eigenvectors of <m>A</m> for distinct eigenvalues. Hence
        we can write <m>\vec{V}_0 = a_1\vec{x}_1 + a_2\vec{x}_2 + a_3\vec{x}_3</m> and
        <md>
          <mrow>
            A^k \vec{V}_0
            \amp = A^k \left( a_1\vec{x}_1 + a_2\vec{x}_2 + a_3\vec{x}_3 \right)
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp = a_1 \left( A^k \vec{x}_1 \right) + a_2 \left( A^k \vec{x}_2 \right)
                 + a_3 \left( A^k \vec{x}_3 \right)
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp = a_1 \lambda_1^k \vec{x}_1 + a_2 \lambda_2^k \vec{x}_2
                 + a_3 \lambda_3^k \vec{x}_3.
          </mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>(I)</term> When <m>k \to \infty</m>, the dominant term is
        <m>a_1 \lambda_1^k \vec{x}_1</m>, since <m>|\lambda_2|, |\lambda_3| \lt 1</m> and
        so those terms decay to zero.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>(II)</term> In this problem
        <m>\vec{V}_0 = \vec{x}_1 + \vec{x}_2 + \vec{x}_3</m>, which means
        <m>a_1 = 1</m>. Therefore
        <md>
          A^k \vec{V}_0 \; \longrightarrow \; (1)(1)^k \begin{bmatrix} 7 \\ 6 \\ 5 \end{bmatrix}
          = \begin{bmatrix} 7 \\ 6 \\ 5 \end{bmatrix}
          \qquad \text{as } k \to \infty.
        </md>
        In the long run the <m>18</m> trucks distribute themselves as <m>7</m>, <m>6</m>,
        and <m>5</m> among the three locations.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <example xml:id="eg-owls">
    <title>An Owl Population</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Consider an owl population of <m>100</m> adult females and <m>40</m> juvenile
        females. As biologists, we wish to study the growth of the population having
        determined the following.
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              The number of juvenile females hatched in any year is twice the number of
              adult females in the previous year.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li><p>Half the adult females in any year survive to the next year.</p></li>
          <li>
            <p>
              One quarter of the juvenile females in any year survive to adulthood.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
        Will the owls survive?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Let <m>a_n</m> be the number of adult females after <m>n</m> years and let
        <m>j_n</m> be the number of juvenile females after <m>n</m> years. The three
        conditions say
        <md>
          j_{n+1} = 2a_n,
          \qquad
          a_{n+1} = \frac{a_n}{2} + \frac{j_n}{4}.
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        Writing the state vector after <m>n</m> years as
        <m>V_n = \begin{bmatrix} a_n \\ j_n \end{bmatrix}</m> with
        <m>V_0 = \begin{bmatrix} 100 \\ 40 \end{bmatrix}</m>, we get
        <md>
          V_{n+1} = \begin{bmatrix} \frac{a_n}{2} + \frac{j_n}{4} \\ 2a_n \end{bmatrix}
          = \begin{bmatrix} \frac{1}{2} \amp \frac{1}{4} \\ 2 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}
            \begin{bmatrix} a_n \\ j_n \end{bmatrix}
          = A V_n.
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        The matrix <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} \frac{1}{2} \amp \frac{1}{4} \\ 2 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}</m>
        has two eigenvalues <m>\lambda_1 = 1</m> and <m>\lambda_2 = -\frac{1}{2}</m>, with
        eigenvectors
        <m>\vec{x}_1 = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}</m> and
        <m>\vec{x}_2 = \begin{bmatrix} -1 \\ 4 \end{bmatrix}</m>. Hence
        <md>
          D = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp -\frac{1}{2} \end{bmatrix},
          \qquad
          P = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp -1 \\ 2 \amp 4 \end{bmatrix},
          \qquad
          P^{-1} = \frac{1}{6} \begin{bmatrix} 4 \amp 1 \\ -2 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}.
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        Therefore
        <md>
          V_n = A^n V_0 = P D^n P^{-1} V_0
          = \frac{1}{6} \begin{bmatrix}
              440 + 160 \left( -\frac{1}{2} \right)^n \\
              880 - 640 \left( -\frac{1}{2} \right)^n
            \end{bmatrix}.
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        We can also approximate <m>V_n</m> directly from the dominant eigenvalue. Writing
        <m>\begin{bmatrix} b_1 \\ b_2 \end{bmatrix} = P^{-1}V_0</m>,
        <md>
          b_1 = \frac{1}{6} \begin{bmatrix} 4 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}
                \begin{bmatrix} 100 \\ 40 \end{bmatrix} = \frac{220}{3},
        </md>
        so
        <md>
          V_n \approx b_1 \lambda_1^n \vec{x}_1
          = \left( \frac{220}{3} \right) (1)^n \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}
          = \begin{bmatrix} \frac{220}{3} \\ \frac{440}{3} \end{bmatrix},
        </md>
        which agrees with our previous result as <m>n \to \infty</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        So the owls survive: since <m>\lambda_1 = 1</m>, the population neither grows
        without bound nor dies out, but settles at roughly <m>73</m> adult and <m>147</m>
        juvenile females.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>
</section>
